            0.064
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Number Play"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. When divided by 7, the number 661 leaves a remainder of 3, and 4779 leaves a remainder of 5. Without calculating, can you say what remainders the following expressions will leave when divided by 7? Show the solution both algebraically and visually."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.166,
            0.688,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) 4779 + 661 (ii) 4779 – 661"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.196,
            0.848,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Find a number that leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 3, a remainder of 3 when divided by 4, and a remainder of 4 when divided by 5. What is the smallest such number? Can you give a simple explanation of why it is the smallest?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.301,
            0.6,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.2 Checking Divisibility Quickly"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.335,
            0.848,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Earlier, you have learnt shortcuts to check whether a given number, written in the Indian number system is divisible by 2, 4, 5, 8, and 10. Let us revisit them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.397,
            0.847,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Divisibility by 10, 5, and 2:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If the units digit of a number is ‘0’, then it is divisible by 10. Let us understand why this works through algebra."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.435,
            0.848,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can write the general form of a number in the Indian system using a set of letter-numbers. For example, a 5-digit number can be expressed as, edcba denoting "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "e \\times 10000 \\pm \\overline{d} \\times 1000 \\pm \\overline{c} \\times 100 + b \\times 10 + a."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The letter-numbers e, d, c, b, and a denote each digit of a 5-digit number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.509,
            0.848,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Any number can be written in general as…dcba, where the letternumbers a, b, c and d represent the units, tens, hundreds and thousands digit, respectively, and so on. As a sum of place values, this number is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.371,
            0.569,
            0.628,
            0.588
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_11_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "+ 1000d + 100c + 10b + a"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.593,
            0.783,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(For example, in the number 4075, d = 4, c = 0, b = 7, and a = 5.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.612,
            0.848,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We know that each place value, with the exception of the units place, is a multiple of 10. So, 10b, 100c, … all will be multiples of 10. Hence, the number will be divisible by 10 if and only if the units digit a is 0."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.678,
            0.846,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Similarly, explain using algebra why the divisibility shortcuts for 5, 2, 4, and 8 work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.721,
            0.848,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us now examine shortcuts to check divisibility by some other numbers and explain why they work!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.774,
            0.523,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A Shortcut for Divisibility by 9"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.811,
            0.847,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Can you say, without actually calculating, which of these numbers are divisible by 9: 999, 909, 900, 90, 990?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.854,
            0.271,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "All of them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 18,
